ed code as important inspection targets, including content that may not yet exist as a conventional executable file. [Data: Entities (50, 441); Relationships (515)]\\n\\nThis gives AMSI a preventive and visibility-oriented role in the endpoint security chain. The available data does not quantify detection rates or establish how consistently scanning is enabled across systems. [Data: Entities (50, 441)]\"},{\"summary\":\"EDR and Windows Defender form the defensive context\",\"explanation\":\"EDR receives AMSI-related events or inspection data through the antimalware scanning channel, establishing a direct relationship between AMSI and endpoint monitoring. [Data: Relationships (242)]\\n\\nAMSI is also discussed alongside Windows Defender, with both associated with ETW events and with the possibility that scanning of managed code may be blocked or bypassed. This indicates that AMSI is one component of a broader defensive telemetry and detection ecosystem rather than a standalone control. [Data: Relationships (65)]\"},{\"summary\":\"AMSI Bypass is the community’s principal evasion capability\",\"explanation\":\"AMSI Bypass is defined as a collection of techniques intended to evade, disable, or circumvent AMSI and related EDR monitoring. Its relationship to AMSI explicitly describes the objective as preventing or weakening inspection of potentially malicious content. [Data: Entities (450); Relationships (563)]\\n\\nThe described techniques include patching or modifying AMSI and monitoring hooks, as well as invoking operating-system functions without passing through hooked functions in ntdll.dll. These capabilities are security-relevant because they target the inspection path itself, although the data does not establish that a particular actor used them. [Data: Entities (450)]\"},{\"summary\":\"AMSI-Hook Patching and AMSI-AV Patch target user-mode instrumentation\",\"explanation\":\"AMSI-Hook Patching is described as modifying or removing user-mode AMSI and EDR hooks, including hooks in ntdll.dll that invoke amsi.dll. The linked relationship explicitly identifies this technique as one implementation or meaning of AMSI Bypass. [Data: Entities (448); Relationships (528)]\\n\\nAMSI-AV Patch is described as functionally similar to AMSI-hook patching and as involving modification of ntdll.dll to interfere with AMSI-based antivirus inspection. Its direct relationship to AMSI-Hook Patching reinforces that these entries belong to the same defense-evasion branch. [Data: Entities (449); Relationships (529)]\"},{\"summary\":\"amsi.dll and NtCreateFile_Hook connect AMSI to API interception\",\"explanation\":\"amsi.dll is identified as the Windows library associated with AMSI and is described in the supplied hooking flow as a loaded or injected module that can patch functions in ntdll.dll or modify the DLL Import Address Table. It is also described as containing an NtCreateFile_Hook implementation that intercepts the NtCreateFile path before execution reaches the operating-system kernel. [Data: Entities (215); Relationships (245)]\\n\\nThe NtCreateFile_Hook entry separately states that the hook intercepts NtCreateFile before the actual system call is executed, and the relationship links the hook to NtCreateFile. This indicates a technical connection between AMSI-related user-mode monitoring and file-operation interception, without proving that the described hook exists on any particular host. [Data: Entities (256); Relationships (287)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The data indicates declining standalone relevance but persistent monitoring risk\",\"explanation\":\"The AMSI description states that AMSI remains an operating-system security interface used by antivirus and EDR products, while its relevance as a standalone user-mode scanning mechanism is described as declining because modern tools increasingly supplement or replace it with behavioral, memory, and endpoint telemetry. [Data: Entities (50)]\\n\\nThe descriptions of AMSI Bypass and AMSI-Hook Patchin